{
  "policy_id": "con-75-deliver-new-gigawatt-power-plant-at",
  "party": "Conservative",
  "policy_title": "Deliver New Gigawatt Power Plant at Wylfa",
  "outcome": "O2",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O2",
    "direction": "mixed",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "long-term",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "Building new nuclear capacity could eventually lower household exposure to volatile fossil-fuel energy prices, but the construction financing model passes costs to energy bills in the meantime, and comparable projects have run far over budget and behind schedule. The net effect on energy bills depends heavily on final costs and whether overruns are absorbed by consumers.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether nuclear construction costs and the RAB financing model ultimately raise or lower household energy bills relative to the counterfactual mix of alternatives depends on final cost outcomes \u2014 Hinkley Point C's 90% cost overrun suggests significant risk.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy commits to delivering a new gigawatt power plant at Wylfa and supporting Hinkley Point and Sizewell projects.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "The Conservative Party will deliver a new gigawatt power plant at Wylfa in North Wales and work with industry to deliver existing projects at Hinkley Point and Sizewell."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The RAB financing model for nuclear charges energy customers a regulated price on bills to help cover capital construction costs.",
        "evidence_id": "E11",
        "quote": "This model allows companies to charge energy customers a regulated price on energy bills to help cover the capital costs of construction."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Critics (advocacy source) argue nuclear power can be 'outstandingly expensive' compared to other generation methods, leading to higher energy bills.",
        "evidence_id": "E40",
        "quote": "Critics like Friends of the Earth Cymru have argued that nuclear power can be \"outstandingly expensive\" compared to other generation methods, leading to higher energy bills."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Hinkley Point C is currently running seven years late and 90% over budget, raising concerns about cost pass-through.",
        "evidence_id": "E26",
        "quote": "Hinkley Point C has faced significant challenges, currently running seven years late and 90% over budget."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Sizewell C's estimated cost is 70% higher than Hinkley Point C's cost at its Final Investment Decision, fuelling debate about value for money.",
        "evidence_id": "E30",
        "quote": "Its estimated cost of \u00a340.5 billion (2024 money) is reported to be 70% higher than Hinkley Point C's estimated cost at its Final Investment Decision, which has led to questions about \"learning\" benefits."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The Wylfa project is viewed as reducing reliance on volatile fossil fuel markets and providing stable, homegrown baseload power, which could stabilise energy costs long-term.",
        "evidence_id": "E22",
        "quote": "The Wylfa SMR project is viewed as a major milestone for Britain's energy security, reducing reliance on volatile fossil fuel markets and providing stable, homegrown, low-carbon baseload power."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The first SMR reactor at Wylfa is expected to start generating electricity around 2030.",
        "evidence_id": "E25",
        "quote": "The first reactor is expected to start generating electricity around 2030."
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "For O2 \u2014 whether people can afford essentials, especially energy bills \u2014 this policy creates a genuine near/long split. In the near term, the RAB model directly charges energy consumers during the construction phase, adding to bills before any generation benefit materialises. Given that Hinkley Point C is 90% over budget and seven years late, and Sizewell C is estimated at 70% above Hinkley's original FID cost, the risk of substantial cost pass-through to consumers is material and evidence-based. Critics (Friends of the Earth Cymru \u2014 advocacy source, weighted accordingly) argue this makes nuclear 'outstandingly expensive' relative to alternatives, though independent assessment of the comparative cost trajectory is not definitively settled in the provided evidence. In the long term, if the projects deliver at projected costs, stable baseload nuclear reduces exposure to volatile fossil fuel price spikes \u2014 the primary driver of the 2021\u20132023 energy cost crisis \u2014 which would benefit household bills. However, the mechanism fires only at scale after operational delivery, which for Wylfa is projected no earlier than ~2030 and for Sizewell C not until 2039. The counterfactual (what alternative generation mix would cost) is not quantified in the evidence. On balance: near-term the RAB model worsens bills; long-term the energy security benefit could improve them, but cost-overrun risk is high and well-evidenced. This genuine two-directional effect, both grounded in cited evidence, warrants 'mixed' at moderate magnitude.",
    "time_split": {
      "near_direction": "worsens",
      "long_direction": "improves",
      "note": "RAB financing charges consumers during construction; operational baseload reduces fossil-fuel price exposure only once plant delivers, likely post-2030."
    },
